    "description": "Occupational health in Iran connects workplace exposure assessment, medical examinations and preventive services with the wider public-health system. Research identifies small workplaces, limited inspection capacity and weak connections between exposure measurements and clinical follow-up as important service challenges.",
    "tags": ["Occupational health", "Workplace prevention", "Primary health care", "Occupational medicine", "Worker health"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:36:30Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:36:30Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Occupational Health in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s workplace health services, their connections with primary care, and documented challenges in prevention and occupational medicine."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "work-and-public-health",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Where work meets public health",
        "content": "Occupational health examines how working conditions affect health and how services can prevent harm. In Iran, its institutional history includes integration with primary health care: the community services intended to provide accessible prevention and basic care. A review published in 2015 described workers’ health houses as an important delivery approach within this network. It also cautioned that geographic reach did not resolve weaknesses in training, private-sector collaboration or service quality. These findings describe an earlier stage of development, rather than establish present-day coverage. [s1]\n\nThis connection matters because workplace health extends beyond a specialist consultation after an injury. The Iranian national occupational-health profile examines inspections, exposure assessment, preventive controls and medical examinations together. Its framework includes chemical agents, noise, vibration, heat, biological hazards and ergonomic risks associated with the design of tasks and equipment. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "services-and-small-workplaces",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The challenge of reaching small workplaces",
        "content": "The IRANOSH study, a national profile of occupational safety and health services, combined workplace information collected between March 2019 and March 2020 with administrative information and expert discussions. It identified small workplaces as a central priority and reported shortages in the workforce responsible for inspection and specialist services. Its results are a historical assessment, not a live register of provision. [s2]\n\nA particularly useful finding concerns the connection between different services. Participating experts questioned whether measurements of harmful workplace factors were consistently translated into medical examinations or effective controls. Recording an exposure and reducing it are different achievements. The study also describes occupational-health activity involving carpet weaving, agriculture, waste management and hospitals, showing why an account confined to large industrial plants would miss important settings. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "clinical-continuity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Occupational medicine and continuity of care",
        "content": "Occupational medicine is the clinical part of this field, including assessment of work-related illness and a person’s ability to undertake particular duties. A Delphi study, which gathered and refined specialist opinions through successive rounds, identified difficulties involving insurance recognition, professional responsibilities and return-to-work examinations. Its participants also called for better training in functional assessment: evaluating what someone can do in relation to the demands of a job. [s3]\n\nThese are reported professional perspectives, not independently measured rates of service failure. They nevertheless help explain why a medical examination alone cannot demonstrate that a workplace prevention system works. The practical questions include whether findings trigger changes, whether care continues after illness, and whether responsibilities are clear. Read alongside the earlier primary-care review, the research points toward stronger links between community access, workplace prevention and clinical follow-up. It does not establish that proposed reforms have been implemented throughout Iran. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
