    "name": "Older People’s Health",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/health/public-health/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Older people’s health in Iran involves medical care, support for everyday functioning and environments that enable continued participation. Research and institutional initiatives highlight the need to connect health services, social support and care at home while distinguishing proposed models from demonstrated service availability.",
    "tags": ["Healthy ageing", "Older people", "Home care", "Long-term care", "Age-friendly environments"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T05:36:30Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T05:36:30Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Older People’s Health in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Iran’s ageing-related health challenges, age-friendly initiatives, and research on coordinated services and long-term care at home."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "health-and-participation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Health, independence and participation",
        "content": "Iran’s response to population ageing spans healthcare and the conditions that allow people to remain active in their communities. The United Nations Population Fund, or UNFPA, presents age-friendly environments as part of this response, linking demographic change with inclusion and cooperation across institutions. This perspective broadens the subject beyond hospital treatment: the relevant question is also whether older people can participate in everyday life and obtain support when their needs change. [s1]\n\nIn January 2025, UNFPA reported the launch of an age-friendly environment initiative by Iran’s State Welfare Organization and partners. The announcement described a rollout in nine provinces and expansion of an existing pilot in Isfahan. This establishes a reported programme milestone. It does not, by itself, establish improvements in accessibility, health outcomes or nationwide service availability. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "coordination-and-access",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why coordination matters",
        "content": "A mixed-methods study of geriatric healthcare provision identified interconnected challenges involving insurance coverage, budgets, facilities and the distribution of staff. It also identified coordination between the Ministry of Health and Medical Education and the State Welfare Organization as a concern. These institutions address overlapping needs, making the connections between their services an important part of the research agenda. [s2]\n\nThe study also reported problems translating the National Policy Document on Ageing into practice. A policy document, an available service and a service that people can actually use are separate forms of evidence. The research helps identify where implementation may falter, but its prioritization of challenges should not be read as a census of every locality. Provincial and local comparisons require additional evidence rather than assumptions that conditions are uniform. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "care-at-home",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Developing care at home",
        "content": "Long-term care supports people whose needs persist beyond an isolated episode of treatment. A study published in February 2025 developed a proposed Iranian model for providing such care at home. It brought together earlier research, interviews, an expert panel and a Delphi exercise, a structured method for refining expert judgments. The framework addresses governance, regulation, financing, workforce management, information and monitoring. [s3]\n\nIts importance lies in treating home care as an organized system requiring oversight and resources. Agreement among experts about a model’s suitability does not demonstrate that it has been funded, delivered or shown to improve patients’ lives. The authors acknowledge geographical limitations associated with work conducted in Tabriz and the potential subjectivity of expert assessment. [s3]\n\nTogether, these sources suggest a useful way to understand the field: examine the environments people live in, the services they can reach and the continuity of support at home. Reported initiatives and proposed frameworks show directions for development; evidence of implementation and outcomes remains essential when judging their practical effects. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
